\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Lemma\nonbreakingspace \ref {lem:coset} for \(d=(p,q)=(3,2)\) and \((x_0,y_0)=(1,1)\), so that \(c_0=qx_0-py_0=-1\) and \(r_k=-1+6k\). Small grey dots are \(\mathbb  {Z}^2\); large dots are the coset \(C=\{(x,y): x\equiv 1 \bmod  3,\ y\equiv 1 \bmod  2\}\), a translate of the sublattice \(3\mathbb  {Z}\times 2\mathbb  {Z}\). Each line \(\ell _{(3,2),r_k}\) meets \(\mathbb  {Z}^2\) \emph  {only} in \(C\), and consecutive points along a line differ by \((p,q)=(3,2)\); as \(k\) ranges over \(\mathbb  {Z}\) the lines partition \(C\). The levels are equally spaced with gap \(pq=6\): a smaller gap would give lines missing \(\mathbb  {Z}^2\) altogether, a larger one would leave points of \(C\) uncovered.}}{5}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:coset}{{3}{5}{Lemma~\ref {lem:coset} for \(d=(p,q)=(3,2)\) and \((x_0,y_0)=(1,1)\), so that \(c_0=qx_0-py_0=-1\) and \(r_k=-1+6k\). Small grey dots are \(\Z ^2\); large dots are the coset \(C=\{(x,y): x\equiv 1 \bmod 3,\ y\equiv 1 \bmod 2\}\), a translate of the sublattice \(3\Z \times 2\Z \). Each line \(\ell _{(3,2),r_k}\) meets \(\Z ^2\) \emph {only} in \(C\), and consecutive points along a line differ by \((p,q)=(3,2)\); as \(k\) ranges over \(\Z \) the lines partition \(C\). The levels are equally spaced with gap \(pq=6\): a smaller gap would give lines missing \(\Z ^2\) altogether, a larger one would leave points of \(C\) uncovered}{figure.3}{}}
